# L §4.2 calendar example, copied (with its sources; len_ahead left out).  2026's row adds 2026-06-22:
# is_business(2026-06-22) is false; 2027 is read under len_rules, where 2027-06-22 (a Tuesday) is a business day.
# add_business(d, n, cal): "n business days after d ..., counting from the day after": after 2026-12-24 come the holiday
# 12-25, a weekend, then Monday 2026-12-28. "The n-th business day of a period p is add_business(start_of(p) - 1 day,
# n, cal)": 01-01 is a holiday, 01-02 (Friday) the first business day of 2026-01. last_business(2026-12, 1):
# 2026-12-31 is a Thursday, no holiday. (Writer 8: first_business and a month-day in added are gone.)
=== m.tally
model t
  tally 2
  on 2026-12-31
  start 2026-01-01
  end 2027-12-31

source synthetic_calendar "a calendar"
source gazette "a gazette"

input len: calendar
version len_rules
  cite synthetic_calendar
  governs 2020..2035
  known 2020-01-01
  len = calendar(weekends [sat, sun], yearly [01-01, 01-06, 05-01, 05-03, 08-15, 11-01, 11-11, 12-25, 12-26])
version len_2026
  cite gazette
  governs 2026
  known 2025-12-01
  replace len = calendar(weekends [sat, sun], yearly [01-01, 01-06, 05-01, 05-03, 08-15, 11-01, 11-11, 12-25, 12-26], added [2026-06-22])

ask q1: is_business(2026-06-22, len), is_business(2027-06-22, len), add_business(2026-12-24, 1, len)
ask q2: add_business(start_of(2026-01) - 1 day, 1, len), last_business(2026-12, 1, len)
=== expect
q1: (false, true, 2026-12-28)
  cut 2026-12-31
q2: (2026-01-02, 2026-12-31)
  cut 2026-12-31
